; Stage 15 W5d (ergonomics.md §3.10): the element-type refinement is SYNTACTIC
; — it fires for an `(array T …)` initializer and nothing else. A bare `:ptr`
; annotation is this language's void*-style erasure hatch: it drops the element
; type AND the nullability claim on purpose, and a general "adopt the init's
; element type" rule would re-route multimethod dispatch (`type-eq` compares
; pointer elements, so a `ptr:i32` argument does not match a `ptr` parameter)
; across every such binding. This fixture pins that boundary: a bare `:ptr`
; bound from an `alloca` stays elem-less, so `aref` on it is still an error.

(import-use "stdio.h")
(defn main ():i32
  (let (p:ptr (alloca i32 4))
    (printf "%d\n" (aref p 0)))
  (return 0))
